l; thus it functions as a wrapper around a foundation model and is excluded.,"The paper introduces sparse autoencoders to interpret and steer genomic language models (DNA sequences), but the abstract contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.","The work introduces sparse autoencoders to interpret, audit, and steer existing genomic language models rather than presenting a new generative architecture; therefore it is an application wrapper without its own generative model.",The abstract presents sparse autoencoders that interpret and steer an existing genomic language model (Nucleotide Transformer) without describing any natural‑language text input/output or a new generative model; thus it functions as a wrapper around a foundation model and is excluded.,"Decode-gLM: Tools to Interpret, Audit, and Steer Genomic Language Models","While genomic language models are enabling the  de novo design of entire genomes, they remain challenging to interpret, limiting their trustworthiness. Here, we show that sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on Nucleotide Transformer activations decompose hidden representations into interpretable biological features without supervision. Across layers and model sizes, SAEs identified over 100 diverse functional annotations encoded in the model’s activations. This included viral regulatory elements such as the CMV enhancer, despite viral genomes being excluded from training data. Tracing this signal revealed contamination in reference databases, demonstrating that interpretability methods can audit training data and identify hidden data leakage. We then show that Meta-SAEs, trained on the decoder weights of another SAE, can identify conceptual hierarchies encoded in the model, including a more abstract feature related to multiple HIV annotations. We confirmed that the features identified by our SAEs were learned during pretraining through probing a randomly initialised model. Finally, we demonstrate that our SAEs allow us to steer model predictions in biologically meaningful ways, showing that we can use an antibiotic-resistance SAE-feature to steer the model toward the A1408G aminoglycoside-resistance mutation in the ribosomal gene 16S rRNA. Together, these results establish SAEs as a method for both discovery and auditing, providing a toolkit for interpretable and trustworthy genomic foundation models. Readers can explore our findings at  https://interpretglm.netlify.app/ .",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,yes,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes sparse autoencoders applied to a genomic language model (Nucleotide Transformer) for interpretation and steering, with no natural-language text component or text‑bio alignment. It uses an existing foundation model, fitting the definition of an application wrapper, and therefore is excluded.","The work introduces sparse autoencoders to interpret genomic language models trained on nucleotide sequences, with no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping; thus it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.","The abstract presents sparse autoencoders as tools to interpret and steer existing genomic language models, without introducing a new generative architecture.","The abstract describes sparse autoencoders applied to a genomic language model (Nucleotide Transformer) for interpretation and steering, with no natural-language text component or text‑bio alignment. It uses an existing foundation model, fitting the definition of an application wrapper, and therefore is excluded."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:97: This study demonstrates that multi-modal MRIs can successfully identify glioblastoma subtypes characterized by deep learning derived radiomic features. Future work will focus on validation with comprehensive molecular and tissue-based profiles, survival outcomes, and treatment responses to support personalized medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a deep learning subtyping of MRI images using Masked Auto-Encoder (MAE), with no natural-language text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative model for text. Thus it is excluded.","The paper uses MRI and deep learning for imaging subtyping; no text/language component or text-bio bridge is present, so it does not meet criteria for a generative text-bio foundational model.",The paper applies a Masked Auto-Encoder for radiomic feature extraction and clustering on MRI data to identify glioblastoma subtypes; it does not describe a generative architecture or any text/biological sequence generation.,"The abstract describes a deep learning subtyping of MRI images using Masked Auto-Encoder (MAE), with no natural-language text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative model for text. Thus it is excluded.",IMG-33. Unveiling glioblastoma heterogeneity with deep learning derived MRI subtyping: insights from the global ReSPOND consortium,"This study aims to identify distinct imaging subtypes of glioblastoma using multi-modal MRIs from the ReSPOND consortium, providing insights into tumor heterogeneity to inform personalized treatment approaches.
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:146:980,Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes integrative analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of natural-language text, text-bio alignment, or generative modeling; thus it fails text component and generative criteria.","No text/language component; the study uses machine learning on clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for subtyping, without any natural-language text or text-bio alignment.","Abstract describes PD subtyping using ML/DL and drug repurposing; no generative model, text generation, or foundation model evidence present.","The abstract describes integrative analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of natural-language text, text-bio alignment, or generative modeling; thus it fails text component and generative criteria.",Identification of Parkinson’s disease PACE subtypes and repurposing treatments through integrative analyses of multimodal data,"Parkinson’s disease (PD) is a serious neurodegenerative disorder marked by significant clinical and progression heterogeneity. This study aimed at addressing heterogeneity of PD through integrative analysis of various data modalities. We analyzed clinical progression data (≥5 years) of individuals with de novo PD using machine learning and deep learning, to characterize individuals’ phenotypic progression trajectories for PD subtyping. We discovered three pace subtypes of PD exhibiting distinct progression patterns: the Inching Pace subtype (PD-I) with mild baseline severity and mild progression speed; the Moderate Pace subtype (PD-M) with mild baseline severity but advancing at a moderate progression rate; and the Rapid Pace subtype (PD-R) with the most rapid symptom progression rate. We found cerebrospinal fluid P-tau/α-synuclein ratio and atrophy in certain brain regions as potential markers of these subtypes. Analyses of genetic and transcriptomic profiles with network-based approaches identified molecular modules associated with each subtype. For instance, the PD-R-specific module suggested STAT3, FYN, BECN1, APOA1, NEDD4, and GATA2 as potential driver genes of PD-R. It also suggested neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, metabolism, PI3K/AKT, and angiogenesis pathways as potential drivers for rapid PD progression (i.e., PD-R). Moreover, we identified repurposable drug candidates by targeting these subtype-specific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a machine learning analysis of clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for PD subtyping, with no mention of natural language text or generative modeling. Excluded because no text component and no generative model.","The paper applies machine learning and deep learning to clinical, genetic, and transcriptomic data for Parkinson's subtyping and drug repurposing; no generative model, natural-language text component, or text-bio bridge is described, so it is out of scope.","The abstract describes subtyping and progression analysis of Parkinson's disease using machine learning and deep learning, with no mention of generative mechanisms, text generation, or biological seq