mework for CMVD from perfusion PET-derived myocardial blood flow data, revealing distinct patient subgroups beyond traditional case-control definitions. This imaging-based stratification substantially improved classification performance alongside plasma proteomics and PRS, achieving AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.73 per class, significantly outperforming binary classifiers and existing clinical models, highlighting the potential of this stratification approach to enable more precise and personalized diagnosis by capturing the underlying heterogeneity of CMVD. This work represents the first application of imaging-based endotyping and the integration of genetic and proteomic data for CMVD risk prediction, establishing a framework for multimodal modeling in complex diseases.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and genetic data with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism, so it fails EC2 and EC3.","The paper develops risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and genetic data; no text/language component or text-bio bridge is described, so it does not meet inclusion criteria for generative text-bio foundational models.","The abstract describes risk prediction models for CMVD using machine/deep learning, with no mention of generative architecture, text generation, or biological sequence generation. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria for generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract describes risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and genetic data with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism, so it fails EC2 and EC3.",Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant 