vance the development of next-generation diagnostic tools that are both clinically actionable and biologically grounded.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract details a multi‑omics image‑classification method for disease diagnosis with no mention of natural‑language text, text‑bio alignment, or any generative mechanism, leading to exclusion under EC3_not_generative.","The abstract discusses integration of multi-omics data for image classification but does not mention any natural-language text component or text-bio mapping. It is presented as a study of computational approaches rather than a new primary model, fitting a review/editorial category.","The abstract focuses on multi-omics integration for image classification without describing any generative architecture (e.g., decoder, autoregressive). It also lacks mention of broad pretraining or transferable representations, indicating no foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract details a multi‑omics image‑classification method for disease diagnosis with no mention of natural‑language text, text‑bio alignment, or any generative mechanism, leading to exclusion under EC3_not_generative.",Multi-omics approaches for image classification in disease diagnosis,"Introduction The integration of multi-omics data for disease diagnosis holds transformative potential in the field of computational biology, especially when applied to the intricate and dynamic interactions between microbial communities and their human hosts. Methods This integrative approach enables to capture diverse biological signals across genomic, transcriptomic, proteomic, and metabolomic layers, providing a more comprehensive understanding of disease mechanisms. In alignment with emerging priorities in disease microbiology, our study addresses a critical and timely need for interpretable, scalable, and biologically robust computational models that can extract