 abstract presents finetuned LLMs and MLLMs for specific tasks, not a new foundational model, so it is an application wrapper. The description generation task provides clear evidence for text component and text-bio bridge.""}",application_wrapper,yes,yes,yes,yes,no,application_wrapper,none,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions from biological data,generation of descriptions for gene functions,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents finetuned LLMs and MLLMs for specific tasks, not a new foundational model, so it is an application wrapper. The description generation task provides clear evidence for text component and text-bio bridge.",PASS|EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,yes,yes,yes,yes,no,application_wrapper,,generation of descriptions for gene functions,generation of descriptions for gene functions from biological data,generation of descriptions for gene functions,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract presents finetuned LLMs and MLLMs for specific tasks, not a new foundational model, so it is an application wrapper. The description generation task provides clear evidence for text component and text-bio bridge.",EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,False,Geneverse: A collection of Open-source Multimodal Large Language Models for Genomic and Proteomic Research,,"The applications of large language models (LLMs) are promising for biomedical and healthcare research. Despite the availability of open-source LLMs trained using a wide range of biomedical data, current research on the applications of LLMs to genomics and proteomics is still limited. To fill this gap, we propose a collection of finetuned LLMs and multimodal LLMs (MLLMs), known as Geneverse, for three novel tasks in genomic and proteomic research. The models in Geneverse are trained and evaluated based on domain-specific datasets, and we use advanced parameter-efficient finetuning techniques to achieve the model adaptation for tasks including the generation of descriptions for gene functions, protein function inference from its structure, and marker gene selection from spatial transcriptomic data. We demonstrate that adapted LLMs and MLLMs perform well for these tasks and may outperform closed-source large-scale models based on our evaluations focusing on both truthfulness and structural correctness. All of the training strategies and base models we used are freely accessible.",2024.0,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions, protein function inference from its structure"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a new collection of finetuned LLMs and MLLMs (Geneverse) for genomic and proteomic tasks that include generating natural-language descriptions from biological data, clearly satisfying text component and text-bio bridge criteria.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,none,none,generation of descriptions for gene functions,"generation of descriptions for gene functions, protein function inference from its structure",not_assessed,clear_include,"The abstract presents a new collection of finetuned LLMs and MLLMs (Geneverse) for genomic and proteomic tasks that include generating natural-language descriptions from biological data, clearly satisfying text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generation of descriptions for gene functions"", ""boundary_case"": ""wrapper_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper presents finetuned LLMs for generative tasks like gene function description, but the models are adapted from existing open-source LLMs, not a new foundation model, placing it at a wrapper boundary.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,generation of descriptions for gene functions,wrapper_boundary,"The paper presents finetuned LLMs for generative tasks like gene function description, but the models are adapted from existing open-source LLMs, not a new foundation model, placing it at a wrapper boundary.",PASS,PASS,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,PASS|PASS,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,no,,,,,,,"The abstract presents a new collection of finetuned LLMs and MLLMs (Geneverse) for genomic and proteomic tasks that include generating natural-language descriptions from biological data, clearly satisfying text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | The paper presents finetuned LLMs for generative tasks like gene function description, but the models are adapted from existing open-source LLMs, not a new foundation model, placing it at a wrapper boundary.",INCLUDE,,,INCLUDE,,,False
./analysis/current_full_deepseek_replicate_agreement/include_unstable_cases_with_agent_outputs.csv:30:3668,A Quantum Edge Federated Graph Transformer for Generative and Causal Digital Twin Healthcare,10.48084/etasr.16010,"The rapid pace of development in healthcare artificial intelligence requires architectures that transcend predictive analytics to better understand causality and generate simulations of patient trajectories. This study proposes the Quantum Edge Federated Graph Transformer (QFGT), a hybrid quantum-inspired approach aimed at empowering causal, generative, and privacy-preserving digital twin healthcare. The model incorporates quantum kernel attention for complex-amplitude feature embedding, graph transformer reasoning mechanism for relational inference over multimodal clinical entities, and federated optimization for secure multi-institutional learning. Quantum-inspired kernel mappings in Hilbert space encode entangled dependencies between laboratory, imaging, genomic, and clinical text data, while a causal regularization layer constrains learned representations to adhere to interpretable cause-and-effect relations learned from structural causal models. The generative digital-twin module uses a diffusion-based latent simulator that predicts personalized trajectories of the disease, and it supports what-if counterfactual interventions. Federated deployment at the edge healthcare nodes enables model training with data decentralization and strict compliance with HIPAA and GDPR privacy regulations. Experimental work on multimodal clinical data shows an accuracy of 98.1% with a mean early-detection window of 7.9 months and F1-scores >0.98 for all diseases, in addition to an increase in minority-cohort recall of 6.5% via equitable quantum-kernel feature sharing. The proposed QFGT framework opens up a new direction for the quantum-inspired, federated, and causally explainable digital twin systems, which lead to trustworthy, proactive, and personal healthcare intelligence at the quantum edge.",2026.0,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""text_bio_bridge_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""clinical text data"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a new hybrid quantum-inspired model (QFGT) that uses multimodal clinical data including clinical text data, but does not clearly describe a substantive text-bio generative bridge; the generative module predicts disease trajectories rather than text-conditioned biological outputs or bio-to-text generation.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,yes,unclear,none,text_bio_bridge_unclear,clinical text data,none,not_assessed,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract presents a new hybrid quantum-inspired model (QFGT) that uses multimodal clinical data including clinical text data, but does not clearly describe a substantive text-bio generative bridge; the generative module predicts disease trajectories rather than text-conditioned biological outputs or bio-to-text generation.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""yes"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""diffusion-based latent simulator"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a new architecture (QFGT) with a diffusion-based generative digital-twin module, clearly indicating a generative model. No wrapper or exclusion criteria apply.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,unclear,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,d