w opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes an imaging system and deep learning classification without any text component or generative modeling, so it is excluded.","The paper describes a multimodal imaging system and deep learning for single-cell morphological profiling, with no mention of text, language, or natural-language components; thus it clearly lacks a text component.","The abstract describes a multimodal imaging system using deep learning for cell-type classification and cell-cycle tracking, not a generative model; no text generation or foundation model characteristics are mentioned.","The abstract describes an imaging system and deep learning classification without any text component or generative modeling, so it is excluded.",Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-chirp-enhanced delay (FACED) is an ultrafast laser-scanning technique that allows for high imaging speed at the scale orders of magnitude greater than the current technologies. However, this speed advantage has only been restricted to bright-field and fluorescence imaging—limiting the variety of image contents and hindering its applicability in image-based bioassay, which increasingly demands rich phenotypic readout at a large scale. Here, we present a new high-speed quantitative phase imaging (QPI) based on time-interleaved phase-gradient FACED image detection. We further integrate this system with a microfluidic flow cytometer platform that enables synchronized and co-registered single-cell QPI and fluorescence imaging at an imaging throughput of 77 000 cells/s with sub-cellular resolution. Combined with deep learning, this platform empowers comprehensive image-based profiling of single-cell biophysical phenotypes that can offer not only sufficient label-free power for cell-type classification but also cell-cycle phase tracking with high accuracy comparable to the gold-standard fluorescence method. This platform further enables correlative, compartment-specific single-cell analysis of the spatially resolved biophysical profiles at the throughput inaccessible with existing QPI methods. The high imaging throughput and content given by this multimodal FACED imaging system could open new opportunities in image-based single-cell analysis, especially systematic analysis that correlates the biophysical and biochemical information of cells, and provide new mechanistic insights into biophysical heterogeneities in many biological processes.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a high‑speed multimodal imaging system with deep‑learning classification but contains no natural‑language text component, text‑bio alignment, or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.","The abstract describes a new high‑speed quantitative phase imaging system and deep‑learning analysis for single‑cell profiling, with no mention of natural‑language text or text‑based conditioning.","The abstract presents a high‑speed multimodal imaging platform with deep‑learning analysis for cell‑type classification and phase tracking, but it does not describe any generative architecture (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion).","The abstract describes a high‑speed multimodal imaging system with deep‑learning classification but contains no natural‑language text component, text‑bio alignment, or generative mechanism, thus it is excluded under EC3_not_generative.",Multimodal FACED imaging for large-scale single-cell morphological profiling,"Free-space angular-