./analysis/current_full_deepseek_replicate_agreement/decision_mismatches.csv:17:                  CLOP-DiT is a computational pipeline that generates synthetic single-cell gene expression profiles from structured biological descriptions (cell type, tissue, organism, marker genes, and disease context). It first learns to align text descriptions with real cell data in a shared mathematical space, then uses a diffusion model to generate new cell states matching a given description. The generated cells capture correct cell-type identity and marker gene patterns, but do not yet reproduce the full cell-to-cell variability seen in real single-cell experiments. This work demonstrates that text-guided single-cell generation is feasible as a proof of concept, opening directions for future simulation and hypothesis-generation tools in biology.",2026.0,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""text_component_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""BiomedBERT text embeddings used for structured metadata fields"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""text–cell alignment via contrastive learning and conditional generation on text embeddings"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""text_component_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper presents a new model (CLOP-DiT) for single-cell generation conditioned on structured metadata fields (cell type, tissue, etc.) using BiomedBERT text embeddings. However, the text component is structured categorical metadata, not free natural language, making it unclear whether it meets the text component criterion.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,unclear,unclear,none,text_component_unclear,BiomedBERT text embeddings used for structured metadata fields,text–cell alignment via contrastive learning and conditional generation on t