./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0224.stderr.log:1779:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0005.prompt.txt:37:- If the abstract only supports wrapper-style use of an existing LLM or language model, set `paper_type=application_wrapper` and `text_bio_bridge_present=no`.
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0224.stderr.log:1780:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0005.prompt.txt:52:[{"record_id":"rec_000091","title":"Single-Cell Cross-Modal Transfer by Adversarial Fine-Tuning of Foundation Models","abstract":"Spatial transcriptomics (ST) is a powerful tool for exploring biological properties dependent on structure, proximity, and interaction in tissue. The methods underpinning ST are developing rapidly but are limited in their ability to profile many thousands of genes at a subcellular scale. Although dissociated from tissue, it is known that the whole-transcriptome readouts of cells in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) retain information about their former in situ neighbourhoods, motivating computational methods to recover it. While paired ST and scRNA-seq datasets are scarce, each modality in its own right is abundantly available. We therefore propose to perform cross-modal translation between unpaired ST and scRNA-seq data. In this work we show that a single-cell foundation model can perform this translation via adversarial fine-tuning. We demonstrate that our method performs favourably against methods built for multi-omics translation.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["arxiv","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000091","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary cross-modal model for spatial transcriptomics and scRNA-seq. The abstract does not describe any text or language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"cross-modal translation between unpaired ST and scRNA-seq data\" via \"a single-cell foundation model\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000091","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract claims cross-modal translation using a single-cell foundation model, but it does not specify a clearly generative mechanism. It also does not provide enough detail on pretraining or broad applicability to confirm foundation-model characteristics.","evidence_snippet":"\"a single-cell foundation model can perform this translation via adversarial fine-tuning\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000095","title":"Decoding murine corneal epithelial specification and homeostasis by single-cell spatial transcriptomics with scRNA-seq enrichment","abstract":"Abstract\n                \n                  Investigation of gene regulatory programs underlying corneal epithelial cell specification and homeostasis is essential for understanding how the cornea maintains vision. Here, we describe the use of true single-cell resolution spatial transcriptomics (ST), enriched with full-tissue single-cell RNAseq (SC), to improve spatial resolution and enhance cell cluster size up to 65-fold and per-cell transcriptomic depth up to 17-fold. This enabled cell type specification across the full differentiation trajectory from limbal stem cells (LSC) to superficial corneal epithelium and identification of an activated signature (\n                  Atf3, Zfp36, Gsta4 and Dapl1\n                  ) marking differentiation-primed states across multiple cell types, including a major activated intermediate epithelium (AIE) population. Validation using ST data from murine corneas at different postnatal ages and multiple human SC datasets confirms a large AIE population, which spatial localization and transcriptomic profiling suggest is an active intermediate state distinct from quiescent wing cells. Sub-clustering further revealed early (\n                  Sox9, Hes1\n                  ), proliferative (\n                  Mki67, Top2a\n                  ) and mature (\n                  Ccdn1, Dapl1\n                  ) transient amplifying cell subpopulations and four LSC subpopulations, including putative active (\n                  Atf3, Socs3, Zfp36\n                  ), quiescent (\n                  Gpha2, Ifitm3, Cd63\n                  ) and\n                  Apoe\n                  -specific. Direct ST-to-SC comparison revealed enhanced axonal processes and genes (\n                  Sema3f, Sema 4d, Pax6\n                  ) and cell-cell adhesion and cell-matrix markers (\n                  Itgb4, Tns4, Tjp3\n                  ) in ST data, suggesting cell dissociation from tissue in SC masks epithelial innervation, adhesion and barrier functions. Our findings identify and localize key transcriptional programs\n                  in situ\n                  , prompting a re-evaluation of epithelial states in scRNA-seq data.","doi":"10.64898/2026.05.08.723186","year":"2026","venue":"bioRxiv","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000095","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary spatial-transcriptomics/scRNA-seq analysis workflow, but the abstract does not describe any text or language model component. It is biological-only screening for this review.","evidence_snippet":"\"single-cell resolution spatial transcriptomics\" enriched with \"full-tissue single-cell RNAseq\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000095","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract describes a spatial transcriptomics/scRNA-seq biology study and validation of cell states, not a generative model. It does not provide foundation-model evidence.","evidence_snippet":"\"We describe the use of true single-cell resolution spatial transcriptomics\"; \"identify and localize key transcriptional programs in situ\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000097","title":"DposFinder: an interpretable transformer model for predicting phage-derived polysaccharide depolymerases and their host capsular serotypes","abstract":"Background Polysaccharide depolymerases derived from phages play a pivotal role in the degradation of bacterial surface polysaccharides, offering a promising strategy to address antibiotic-resistant bacterial strains. However, the identification of these enzymes along with their associated host capsular serotypes poses significant challenges. This study introduces DposFinder, a transformer-based deep learning framework designed to predict depolymerases alongside their host capsular serotypes, thereby facilitating large-scale discovery and application of these enzymes. Methods We developed DposFinder as an interpretable model based on transformer architecture, utilizing the pre-trained protein language model ESM-2. The model’s attention mechanism was harnessed to pinpoint β-helix domains of depolymerases and predict their corresponding host capsular serotypes. DposFinder’s performance was evaluated through extensive benchmarking and experimental validation involving sequenced phages of Klebsiella pneumoniae. Results DposFinder achieved state-of-the-art performance, with an area under the ROC curve (AUC) of 0.991 on an independent test dataset, outperforming existing depolymerase prediction tools. Experimental validation confirmed the predictions of six novel depolymerases, which exhibited less than 50% sequence identity with known depolymerases. Furthermore, DposFinder successfully predicted the host capsular serotypes for the identified depolymerases. The utility of the model was further demonstrated through improved plasmid electroporation efficiency in hypermucoviscous K. pneumoniae isolates and the facilitation of host-serotype-labeled phage library construction. A publicly accessible database was also established, containing over 100,000 putative depolymerases derived from more than 440,000 phage genomic sequences. Conclusions DposFinder is a novel framework capable of predicting both depolymerases and their associated host capsular serotypes. Its interpretative capabilities and scalability render it a powerful tool for expediting the discovery and application of phage-derived depolymerases in biomedical research and phage therapy. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s13073-026-01657-3.","doi":"10.1186/s13073-026-01657-3","year":"2026","venue":"Genome Medicine","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000097","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A transformer model predicts phage-derived depolymerases and host capsular serotypes from protein sequences using ESM-2. The abstract does not support a substantive text/language component beyond protein modeling.","evidence_snippet":"\"transformer-based deep learning framework\" using \"the pre-trained protein language model ESM-2\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000097","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The model is described as a transformer-based predictor for depolymerases and serotypes, not a generative architecture. It uses a pre-trained protein language model (ESM-2), but the abstract does not show that DposFinder itself has foundation-model-like pretraining or broad transfer.","evidence_snippet":"\"transformer-based deep learning framework\" and \"utilizing the pre-trained protein language model ESM-2\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000098","title":"Leveraging Large Language Models for Single-Cell Foundation Models: Gene Expression Prompting for Cell Type Classification","abstract":"-scale pretrained deep language model for cell type annotation of single-cell rna-seq data,”   , “scgpt: toward building a foundation model for single-cell multi-omics using generative ai,”","doi":"10.1109/icassp55912.2026.11462727","year":"2026","venue":"IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing","sources":["google_scholar","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000098","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"The title indicates large language models applied to single-cell gene-expression prompting for cell type classification. The abstract excerpt is too truncated to confirm the exact text-bio bridge or whether the method is a full candidate model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"Leveraging Large Language Models for Single-Cell Foundation Models: Gene Expression Prompting for Cell Type Classification\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000098","paper_type":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"paper_type_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record is too underspecified to determine whether it is a primary model paper, review, or another article type. The abstract fragment does not clearly describe a generative mechanism or foundation-model properties.","evidence_snippet":"Title mentions \"Single-Cell Foundation Models\" and the abstract text is truncated to citation fragments only"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000099","title":"PhAME: Phenotype-Aware Molecular Editing via Latent Diffusion","abstract":"Small-molecule drug discovery requires simultaneous optimization of numerous properties of candidate molecules. These properties can be investigated through the analysis of high-dimensional biological signatures, such as cell morphology and transcriptomic perturbations, which provide a rich perspective on the underlying biological mechanisms. However, existing generative methods, which use those signatures for optimization, fail to meet two key requirements: providing precise guidance toward desired phenotypic signatures while maintaining structural proximity to a known hit. We introduce PhAME (Phenotype-Aware Molecular Editing), a latent diffusion framework that overcomes this challenge by recasting molecular optimization as editing in the latent space of a pretrained graph-based VAE. Our central contribution is a compositional classifier-free guidance scheme with two independent scales, one for the phenotype-conditioning and one for similarity to the seed structure, allowing practitioners to control the tradeoff between these two objectives. Empirical evaluations across diverse benchmarks, including docking score optimization and multimodal phenotypic generation, demonstrate that PhAME achieves state-of-the-art results while maintaining high chemical validity and novelty.","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000099","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"PhAME edits molecules using biological signatures such as cell morphology and transcriptomic perturbations. No text or language component is described in the model itself.","evidence_snippet":"\"cell morphology and transcriptomic perturbations\"; \"latent diffusion framework\" for molecular editing"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000099","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly describes a latent diffusion framework, which is a genuinely generative architecture. It mentions a pretrained graph-based VAE, but does not establish broad pretraining or other clear foundation-model-like characteristics for PhAME itself.","evidence_snippet":"\"latent diffusion framework\" and \"latent space of a pretrained graph-based VAE\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000100","title":"NLP-Driven Gradient Boosting Ensemble Framework for Automated Viral Genome Classification in Large-Scale Metagenomic Data","abstract":"The rapid advancement of next-generation sequencing (NGS) technologies has generated unprecedented volumes of metagenomic data, yet accurate identification of viral sequences remains a significant computational challenge. This study presents an advanced machine learning framework integrating Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques with state-of-the-art gradient boosting algorithms for automated viral genome classification. Seven distinct machine learning algorithms were evaluated on a large-scale dataset of 214,000 genomic sequences from NCBI GenBank, RefSeq, and IMG/VR. CatBoost [5] achieved the highest individual accuracy of 92.3% (precision 0.924, recall 0.909). The weighted ensemble model achieved 92.8% accuracy, F1-score 0.921, and AUC-ROC 0.9673. Key contributions include: (1) SMOTE [7] achieving 24.6% improvement in balanced accuracy by generating 32,523 synthetic minority samples; (2) multi-scale k-mer features with 4-mers and 5-mers accounting for $\\mathbf{6 2. 9 \\%}$ of discriminative power; (3) consistent 5-fold cross-validation with $\\sigma<0.01$ confirming generalization; (4) benchmark performance metrics establishing gradient boosting superiority over traditional and deep learning viral classification methods for large-scale metagenomic analysis.","doi":"10.1109/RMKMATE69073.2026.11518573","year":"2026","venue":"2026 3rd International Conference on Research Methodologies in Knowledge Management, Artificial Intelligence and Telecommunication Engineering (RMKMATE)","sources":["semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000100","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an automated viral genome classification pipeline using NLP techniques and gradient boosting on genomic k-mer features. It reads as a downstream wrapper/classifier, not a substantive text-bio foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrating Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques\" with \"gradient boosting algorithms\" for viral genome classification"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000100","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a gradient-boosting ensemble for viral genome classification, which is predictive rather than generative. The abstract provides no evidence of foundation-model-style pretraining or transferable representations.","evidence_snippet":"\"automated viral genome classification\" and \"gradient boosting ensemble model\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]
